{
  "abstract": "A machine-checked proof of the Kochen-Specker theorem: for every d at least 3 and for real or complex scalars (any RCLike field), no map from the vectors of the d-dimensional Hilbert space to {true, false} is true on exactly one vector of every orthonormal basis. The map is defined on all vectors, and nothing is assumed about its values off unit vectors or under phases. Dimension three uses Peres's 33 rays, coordinates in {0, 1, -1, sqrt 2, -sqrt 2}, kept exactly as integer pairs; their 72 orthogonal pairs and 16 orthogonal triads admit no colouring, which a case-split certificate generated by a script and checked by the Lean kernel shows. A valuation in dimension d + 1 restricts to the orthogonal complement of a unit vector on which it is false, so the theorem passes to every larger dimension, in any inner product space. The case d at least 4 is also proved without computer search, from Cabello, Estebaranz and Garcia-Alcaine's 18 vectors by their parity count. A valuation on R^2 is also constructed, so the hypothesis d at least 3 is needed. Earlier formal proofs cover R^3 (Lean, HOL Light) and C^4 (Lean); this entry gives the statement for every dimension at least 3, over R and C at once. The theorem is on Mathlib's list of 1000+ theorems (docs/1000.yaml, Wikidata Q528955), with no formalization recorded there.",
